          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Depth-first over the AST, parallel across siblings",
            "A `[Post]` of 50 calls `author` 50 separate times",
            "Each call is blind to its 49 siblings",
            "That blindness is why naive code re-queries"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Batching Window"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Resolvers may return promises, so all 50 siblings start before any completes. They fire in one tick of the event loop — that shared tick is where a batcher collects keys."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Where To Hang Things",
          "items": [
            "`context`: per-request, natural home for loaders",
            "`info`: the AST, for projection optimizations",
            "Property access on a loaded parent is free",
            "A network call per field is not"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "No Free Coordination"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The executor coordinates nothing between resolvers",
            "Batching comes from you or a library",
            "Tree walk where each node may do I/O",
            "DataLoader makes that I/O per level, not per node"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Once Per Parent",
          "text": "The common misread is 'my resolver only runs once'. It runs once per parent object, not once per query."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A per-request utility that coalesces many `.load(key)` calls into one `batchLoadFn(keys)` call. Facebook's, roughly 400 lines, transport-agnostic — SQL, Redis, HTTP, gRPC, anything keyed."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const byId = new DataLoader(async ids => {",
            "  const rows = await db.users.whereIn(ids)",
            "  const m = new Map(rows.map(r => [r.id, r]))",
            "  return ids.map(id => m.get(id) ?? null)",
            "})"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "API Surface"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`load(k)`",
              "queue key, return promise"
            ],
            [
              "`loadMany(ks)`",
              "array, may hold Errors"
            ],
            [
              "`clear(k)`",
              "drop one cache entry"
            ],
            [
              "`clearAll()`",
              "drop whole cache"
            ],
            [
              "`prime(k,v)`",
              "seed cache, no fetch"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Same Length, Same Order",
          "text": "The batch function must return one slot per key, in key order. `WHERE id IN (...)` returns arbitrary order and omits misses — always remap through a `Map`."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Constructor",
          "items": [
            "`new DataLoader(batchFn, options)`",
            "`batchFn: (keys) => Promise<(V|Error)[]>`",
            "Keys in, same arity out — always"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Batch Function Slot Rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Slot Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`load()` returns a promise now, fetches nothing yet",
            "Missing key: put `null` or an `Error` in the slot",
            "Never silently shorten the returned array",
            "An `Error` slot rejects only that key's promise"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "DataLoader: How a Batch Forms",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shape Of A Batch"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- 50 .load(authorId) calls become:",
            "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id IN (1..12);",
            "-- 12 keys: dupes merged at queue time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`loadMany` never rejects, unlike `Promise.all`",
            "Not a fetching library — it wraps your fetch",
            "One loader per entity and access pattern",
            "`userById`, `postsByAuthorId`, never a god loader"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How A Batch Forms",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "`.load(k)`",
              "queue push",
              "nextTick",
              "dispatch",
              "one batchFn"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`.load()` pushes key, resolve, reject onto a queue",
            "Schedules a dispatch if none is pending",
            "Default scheduler is `process.nextTick` in Node",
            "It fires after the sync frame drains",
            "Loads in a later tick form a new batch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why It Fits GraphQL"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Sibling resolvers all fire synchronously in the same tick, before any await resolves. Two `.load(7)` calls in that tick share one promise and one key in the batch."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Killing Your Own Batch"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "for (const id of ids) await load(id) // bad",
            "await Promise.all(ids.map(id => load(id)))",
            "await other() // later loads = new batch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Option",
            "Effect",
            "Set when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`batchScheduleFn`",
              "widen window",
              "cross-tick loads"
            ],
            [
              "`maxBatchSize`",
              "chunks batch",
              "driver param cap"
            ],
            [
              "`batch: false`",
              "cache only",
              "debug, 1-key source"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Parameter Caps",
          "items": [
            "Postgres: about 65535 bind params",
            "SQL Server: 2100 params",
            "`fn => setTimeout(fn, 10)` widens across ticks",
            "Chunking is not optional on big key sets"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Per-Level Batching & the Request Cache",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Depth Costs, Not Breadth"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Level 2 dispatches after level 1 resolves",
            "Batching is per level, never global",
            "Depth D costs D batch calls",
            "Sequential await gives N batches of 1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "D Trips, Not N^D",
          "text": "Naive nesting issues N^D queries. Per-level batching walks the same tree in D round trips."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cache Before Queue",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The second job: memoization keyed by the `load()` key, checked before anything is queued. A hit returns the same promise object, so a repeat `.load(7)` costs zero extra I/O."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Diamond"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "user 7",
            "rows": [
              [
                "n0"
              ],
              [
                "b0",
                "b1",
                "b2"
              ],
              [
                "c0_0"
              ],
              [
                "c1_0"
              ],
              [
                "c2_0"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "n0": {
                "text": "user 7",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "b0": {
